          %title Initiality Project - Raw Syntax
          %h1#initiality_project__raw_syntax Initiality Project - Raw Syntax
          %p
            This page is part of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Initiality+Project"} Initiality Project
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#variables_and_metavariables"} Variables and meta-variables
              %li
                %a{:href => "#sorts_operators_and_constants"} Sorts, operators, and constants
              %li
                %a{:href => "#raw_terms"} Raw terms
              %li
                %a{:href => "#operators"} Operators
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#types"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Π
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
                    \-types
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#others"} Others
              %li
                %a{:href => "#structural_induction"} Structural induction
              %li
                %a{:href => "#equivalence"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
                  \-equivalence
              %li
                %a{:href => "#weakening_exchange_and_contraction"} Weakening, exchange, and contraction
              %li
                %a{:href => "#substitution"} Substitution
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Here we define the “raw syntax” for our type theory. These are sets whose elements are called “raw terms” and “raw types”. They are obtained by putting together operations like “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            \-types” and “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            \-abstraction” in a way that “makes sense locally” but not necessarily globally.
          %p
            In traditional approaches to logic, one often defines the raw terms (or “well-formed formulas”) as a subset of the set of all “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+%28computer+science%29"}> strings
            ”, i.e. finite lists of elements of some “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alphabet"}> alphabet
            ” set such as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Π
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo :
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\Sigma,\Pi,\lambda,(,),:,\dots \}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A\times B)
            so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ((A\times B)\times C)
            has an unambiguous meaning.
          %p
            However, a more modern perspective is to regard this as defining an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
            (in the metatheory), with each clause as a constructor. In this case, the raw terms themselves are not lists of symbols with some property, but rather
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trees"} trees
            whose nodes are labeled with appropriate symbols. This way we obtain automatically an induction principle for defining functions by recursion and proving theorems by induction over the set of terms, which in the strings-of-symbols approach one has to prove by hand. The strings of symbols we write on the page like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A\times B)\times C
            are then regarded as just a
            %em notation
            for these “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+syntax+tree"}> abstract syntax trees
            ”.
          %p
            It’s true that to be completely precise about the
            %em entire
            passage from “mathematics written on the page” to its meaning, one would want to also define the strings of symbols and prove that they faithfully represent the syntax trees. This is analogous to how a compiler for a programming language must start out by “parsing” and “lexing” the code written by the programmer (which is a string of symbols) into an internal “abstract syntax tree” representation. However, such things are well-understood and extremely uninteresting, so much so that programmers often use “parser generator” programs to
            = succeed ";" do
              %em write parsers for them
          %h2#variables_and_metavariables Variables and meta-variables
          %p
            Experts will know that there are many ways to deal with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+variable"} variable binding
            in type theory, including “named variables”, “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Bruijn+indices"}> de Bruijn indices
            ”, “de Bruijn levels”, “locally nameless variables”, and so on. We will use “named variables”. The reason is that our goal is expositional and sociological, and named variables keep the syntax as close as possible to what “users” of type theory are familiar with (e.g. with named variables we write
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi λ
                    %mi y
                    %mo .
                    %mi App
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x. \lambda y. App(x,y)
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo .
                    %mi λ
                    %mo .
                    %mi App
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda. \lambda. App(2,1)
            %em really
            using them.
          %p
            Thus, we suppose given an infinite set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Var
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Var
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} Constructivists
            should assume that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Var
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Var
            has
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+equality"} decidable equality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi fr
                  %mo :
                  %mi Var
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi fin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Var
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Var
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fr : Var \times P_{fin}(Var) \to Var
            which replaces a variable with another one that is “fresh for”, i.e. distinct from all of, a given finite set of variables. That is, we assume that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi fr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∉
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fr(x,V) \notin V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∉
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\notin V
            then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi fr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fr(x,V) = x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi fr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fr(x,V)
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^{[V]}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \{x_1,\dots,x_n\}
            is a linearly ordered set of variables we will write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msubsup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msubsup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{[V]} = \{ x_1^{[V]}, x_2^{[V\cup \{x_1^{[V]}\}]}, \dots, x_n^{[V \cup \{x_1,\dots,x_{n-1}\}^{[V]}]}\}
          %p
            for the result of freshening each element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in succession, ensuring that they remain distinct not only from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            but also from each other.
          %p
            We will also frequently use
            = succeed "." do
              %em meta-variables
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Var
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Var
          %h2#sorts_operators_and_constants Sorts, operators, and constants
          %p
            Overall, we are not attempting in this project to treat “all type theories” at any level of generality, instead simply dealing with individual type formers one by one (though attempting to be as “modular” as possible). However, at the level of
            %em raw
            syntax we can easily be fully general, thereby saving ourselves some additional work in each case. Thus, we will work with a variant of what in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Practical+Foundations+for+Programming+Languages"} Practical Foundations for Programming Languages
            are called
            = succeed "," do
              %em abstract binding trees
            %em sorts
            and a set of
            = succeed "." do
              %em operators
          %p
            For us the set of
            %strong sorts
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sort
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi tm
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ty
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sort = \{ tm, ty \}
          %p
            The set of
            %strong operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op
            is a parameter of the theory; the rules for each type former begin by specifying some relevant operators. For instance, the operators associated to
            = succeed "-types" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi Π
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi App
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ \Pi, \lambda, App\}
          %p
            Each operator is furthermore associated with a
            %strong signature
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong generalized arity
          %ol
            %li
              A sort (i.e. either
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi tm
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tm
              or
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ty
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ty
            %li
              A natural number
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "." do
                %strong argument arity
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]=\{1,\dots,n\}
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sort
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sort
              = succeed "." do
                %strong argument sorts
            %li
              A natural number
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              = succeed "." do
                %strong binding arity
            %li
              A decidable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lhd
              between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [m]
              called
              = succeed "." do
                %strong scoping
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo ⊲
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\lhd k
              we say that the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi j
                      %mi th
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j^{th}
              argument is
              %em in the scope of
              the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi k
                      %mi th
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^{th}
              binder.
          %p
            Often (e.g. in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PFPL"} PFPL
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [m]
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            = succeed "-abstraction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo .
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda(x:A.B).M
            it is natural to consider
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            to be both in the scope of the variable
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                  %mo .
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda(A,x.B,x'.M)
            in which potentially-different variables are bound in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            as an operator with 3 arguments, two of sort
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ty
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ty
            and one of sort
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi tm
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tm
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ⊲
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\lhd 1
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ⊲
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3\lhd 1
          %p
            Fully general abstract binding trees also allow each
            %em binder
            to be associated to a sort. However, in our type theories all variables will range only over terms (not types).
          %p
            Finally, in addition to the operators arising from type formers, we assume a collection of
            = succeed "," do
              %strong constants
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and all arguments having sort
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi tm
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tm
          %p
            We will usually use single upper-case typewriter font letters like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "monospace"}
                    %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "monospace"}
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathtt{C},\mathtt{D}
            for meta-variables ranging over operators, including constants.
          %h2#raw_terms Raw terms
          %p
            Our raw syntax, though untyped, will be
            = succeed "." do
              %em strongly scoped
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Raw
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Raw
            indexed by
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi fin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Var
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Sort
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{fin}(Var) \times Sort
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                raw terms/types with variables from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For any
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mi fin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Var
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\in P_{fin}(Var)
                and any
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in V
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Raw
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo ,
                        %mi tm
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in Raw(V,tm)
            %li
              %p
                Suppose
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "monospace"}
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathtt{C}
                is an operator of sort
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                and binding arity
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi fin
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Var
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V, X\in P_{fin}(Var)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi X
                        %mo =
                        %mi ∅
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\cap X = \emptyset
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo =
                        %mi m
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|X|} = m
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \{x_1,\dots, x_m\}
                totally ordered. Given
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in [n]
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mi j
                      %mo ⊲
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_j = \{x_k \mid j \lhd k \}
                for the variables bound in argument
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in [n]
                we have
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi M
                          %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Raw
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo ∪
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi j
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi t
                          %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_j\in Raw(V\cup X_j, t_j)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi Sort
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t:[n] \to Sort
                gives the argument sorts of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "monospace"}
                          %mi C
